module

public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Properties
public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.UnderlyingMap
public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.Finite
public import Mathlib.Topology.Sober

import Mathlib.RingTheory.IntegralClosure.Algebra.Basic
import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.QuasiSeparated
import WeilConjectures.AlgebraicGeometry.SpecFieldPointResidueField

/-! # An integral affine cover with compact generic-point subspace

Source: David Rydh, Submersions and effective descent of étale morphisms,
Definition (3.8), Properties (3.9)(iii),(vi), and the affine case of Theorem (3.10),
printed pages 13–14 (blueprint: lem-sfb-affine-integral-compact-generics-cover).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Every affine scheme has a reduced affine integral surjective cover whose generic-point
subspace is quasi-compact. No reducedness, Noetherianity or nonemptiness is assumed of the base.

Source: Rydh, Submersions and effective descent of étale morphisms,
Definition (3.8), Properties (3.9)(iii),(vi), and the affine case of Theorem (3.10),
printed pages 13–14 (blueprint: lem-sfb-affine-integral-compact-generics-cover).

Proof plan:
1. Write `Y = Spec A`. Take the product `P` of the residue fields of all primes of `A`,
   using lem-weil-pbc-spec-field-point-residue-field for the canonical maps with kernel
   the given prime. Let `B` be the integral closure of `A` in `P`
   (def-weil-pbc-integral-closure). It contains the image of `A`; set `C = Spec B` and
   let `p` be the spectrum of this factorization (def-weil-affine-spectrum).
2. A subring of a product of fields is reduced. Its localizations remain reduced:
   if `(b/s)^n = 0`, some `t` outside the prime satisfies `t*b^n = 0`, whence
   `(t*b)^n = 0`, so `t*b = 0`. The stalk description from
   lem-weil-pbc-affine-open-stalk-localization therefore gives `IsReduced C`.
3. Apply lem-sfb-integral-affine-cover-criterion to the one-member affine cover of `Y`.
   The inverse image is affine and every element of `B` is integral over `A`, so `p`
   is integral. This does not require `A` to be reduced or Noetherian.
4. Restrict each coordinate projection of `P` to `B`. The resulting morphism from the
   residue-field spectrum to `C` maps under `p` to the original prime of `A`, by
   lem-weil-pbc-spec-field-point-residue-field and functoriality of Spec. Thus `p`
   is surjective, even when `A` has nilpotents.
5. For `b` in `B`, let `e_b` have coordinate `1` precisely where the coordinate of `b`
   is zero, and `0` elsewhere. It satisfies `T^2 - T = 0`, so lies in `B`. Coordinatewise,
   `b*e_b = 0`, and `b*c = 0` holds exactly when `c = e_b*c`. Hence the annihilator
   of `b` is generated by this idempotent.
6. By lem-sfb-idempotent-annihilator-generic-retraction, `C` retracts continuously
   onto `genericPoints C`. The affine scheme `C` is quasi-compact by
   lem-weil-pbc-affine-scheme-qcqs. Pull an open cover back along the retraction,
   take a finite subcover on `C`, and use the identity on the retract to obtain a
   finite subcover of its generic-point subspace. For empty `Y`, the product and
   its integral-closure subring are the zero ring; `C` and its generic-point subspace
   are empty, so the same conclusion holds.
-/
theorem exists_affine_integral_surjective_compact_genericPoints (Y : Scheme.{u}) [IsAffine Y] :
    ∃ (C : Scheme.{u}) (p : C ⟶ Y),
      IsReduced C ∧ IsAffine C ∧ Surjective p ∧ IsIntegralHom p ∧
        CompactSpace (genericPoints C) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
